WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:11.430
- Hi, I'm Caitlin.

00:00:11.430 --> 00:00:13.160
I'm gonna be talking a bit today

00:00:13.160 --> 00:00:18.160
about data viz, data visualization
and the spread of COVID.

00:00:18.590 --> 00:00:19.990
So, this is something

00:00:19.990 --> 00:00:22.850
that pretty much everyone's
been inundated with

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:24.373
over the past year or so.

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:26.800
I feel like every day

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.190
there's a different version
of a graph surrounding COVID,

00:00:30.190 --> 00:00:31.410
something about the virus,

00:00:31.410 --> 00:00:35.530
some new way to break
down how it's spreading

00:00:35.530 --> 00:00:38.260
and allowing us to kinda
talk about it a bit more.

00:00:38.260 --> 00:00:40.510
And so, today I'm gonna
actually just break down a bit

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:45.510
how data viz and graphs in
general are sort of used

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.770
by society at large,

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:52.230
why they're being used at all,

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:56.330
and how people sort of
use different practices

00:00:56.330 --> 00:01:01.233
to actually talk about
the spread of COVID-19.

00:01:02.360 --> 00:01:04.070
So, first off,

00:01:04.070 --> 00:01:06.650
I think really quickly to
start off this conversation,

00:01:06.650 --> 00:01:11.650
data viz is this huge,
expensive sort of field,

00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:16.236
but it touches on all
kinds of disciplines.

00:01:16.236 --> 00:01:17.730
It's data science,

00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:19.410
it's color theory,

00:01:19.410 --> 00:01:22.170
it's design practices as well, right?

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:25.060
So it's intersecting a bunch
of different disciplines.

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:28.810
So I'm only gonna touch
on some of these aspects,

00:01:28.810 --> 00:01:31.040
but just know that there's a
really broad and rich community

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.113
of people that work in data visualization.

00:01:35.740 --> 00:01:36.573
But at its core,

00:01:36.573 --> 00:01:39.100
data visualization is a way to communicate

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:41.450
and make sense of information.

00:01:41.450 --> 00:01:42.950
And so, as people have been going through

00:01:42.950 --> 00:01:45.863
this very challenging
global health crisis,

00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:52.070
we crave narratives and stories
to process this information

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.210
and to help us understand
what's happening.

00:01:55.210 --> 00:01:56.923
And so data visualizations,

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:00.740
allow us to make sense

00:02:00.740 --> 00:02:03.080
of this massive information
we're being inundated with

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:05.430
really quickly and efficiently.

00:02:05.430 --> 00:02:06.430
And so this is the main reason

00:02:06.430 --> 00:02:08.993
why we would visualize
the spread of COVID-19.

00:02:11.390 --> 00:02:15.053
And the first big example of this,

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:19.690
was the Flatten the Curve graphic.

00:02:19.690 --> 00:02:20.837
There are couple versions of this,

00:02:20.837 --> 00:02:23.675
this is sort of like
really nice cartoon version

00:02:23.675 --> 00:02:26.640
that I liked that was put out early on

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:30.090
at the start of the
pandemic around March or so.

00:02:30.090 --> 00:02:31.290
And it was meant to symbolize

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:35.240
how public health practices
like social distancing,

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:40.240
and staying home when sick
could prevent a surge in cases,

00:02:40.420 --> 00:02:44.100
which would exceed the capacity
of our healthcare system,

00:02:44.100 --> 00:02:47.173
so that's the sort of dotted
line is indicating here.

00:02:48.405 --> 00:02:51.230
And this actual graphic
was a collaboration

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:54.770
between infectious disease
experts and illustrators

00:02:54.770 --> 00:02:59.770
to convey what's actually
a very complex idea

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.750
as simply and clearly as possible, right?

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:03.603
There was a point to this, right?

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:09.500
It was to drive home
why adopting practices

00:03:09.690 --> 00:03:12.980
like social distancing are so important,

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:13.813
and then ultimately,

00:03:13.813 --> 00:03:15.890
sort of encouraging social responsibility.

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.610
We're not getting into the how

00:03:19.610 --> 00:03:22.720
but much more of the why
of adopting the practices,

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:24.080
but that's the narrative of this viz,

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.286
and it's extremely compelling.

00:03:25.286 --> 00:03:28.290
I'm sure most people have
interfaced with a version of this

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:32.260
and have heard this sort of
phrase, "Flatten the curve"

00:03:32.260 --> 00:03:33.480
at multiple junctures,

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:36.003
especially early on during the pandemic.

00:03:37.870 --> 00:03:39.990
If a visualization's generally,

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:42.490
it's purpose is to convey a narrative,

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:43.323
we have to remember

00:03:43.323 --> 00:03:46.773
that there are many different
narratives out there,

00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:51.610
some led by the data and
some led by an agenda.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:55.950
And so there's a huge responsibility,

00:03:55.950 --> 00:04:00.640
especially for something
like an epidemic or pandemic

00:04:02.594 --> 00:04:05.710
in the way we communicate
information about it.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:07.000
And so visualizations are powerful

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:09.320
for communicating information,

00:04:09.320 --> 00:04:12.120
but they can also mislead, misinform,

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:13.470
and in worst cases

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.310
for something like a
disease, incite panic.

00:04:16.310 --> 00:04:19.270
And so, we're in the middle
of this information overload

00:04:19.270 --> 00:04:22.520
with hourly case updates,
endless streams of information.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:23.660
So people are even more likely

00:04:23.660 --> 00:04:26.019
to latch on to distilled viz, right?

00:04:26.019 --> 00:04:27.400
We're already sort of primed

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:29.460
we need to have some sort of structure

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.173
amidst the chaos of info.

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:35.160
And so, we're ready to latch onto stuff

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:36.743
that just seems very simple.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:39.750
Let me move myself out
of the way actually,

00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:42.360
so you can see these wonderful...

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:45.800
I'm just gonna put myself in the midst

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:49.563
of this fun party.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:53.310
I don't know if I would go
to dinner with these people,

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:54.463
but here we are.

00:04:55.520 --> 00:05:00.520
Governor Cuomo in the
upper right of this page.

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:06.050
This is a screengrab, again,
early on in the pandemic.

00:05:06.050 --> 00:05:10.470
He was doing these kind of
regular press briefings,

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:13.740
updating on the state of cases

00:05:13.740 --> 00:05:15.460
and the spread of COVID in New York.

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:18.810
And so, this is a very
cluttered sort of table here,

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:23.670
but he is at least talking about
multiple different metrics.

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:25.160
Each one of these columns
is the different metric

00:05:25.160 --> 00:05:28.830
including things like
hospitalization increases

00:05:28.830 --> 00:05:30.120
over the past period of time,

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:32.503
I think he's talking about the past week,

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:35.330
ICU bed availability.

00:05:35.330 --> 00:05:40.330
So, were space in
critical care facilities,

00:05:40.360 --> 00:05:45.360
departments in hospitals,
rates of testing happening.

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:50.140
So there are many different
metrics he's comparing across,

00:05:50.140 --> 00:05:53.300
and the kind of rows here
indicating different regions

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:54.443
of New York State,

00:05:55.950 --> 00:05:58.702
but this is not comprehensive, right?

00:05:58.702 --> 00:06:00.420
These are only a handful

00:06:00.420 --> 00:06:03.700
of all possible important
metrics to consider

00:06:03.700 --> 00:06:07.720
when deciding, for example,
as someone who's a policymaker

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:08.640
for the state,

00:06:08.640 --> 00:06:10.440
where to allocate ventilator supplies,

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:13.880
or where to impose travel restrictions.

00:06:13.880 --> 00:06:15.560
And these metrics were probably chosen

00:06:15.560 --> 00:06:17.860
to support the Department of Health's plan

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:20.740
to classify different regions
as maybe a hotspot or not,

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:23.820
or a place where maybe
there is a higher need

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:24.690
for more ventilators

00:06:24.690 --> 00:06:26.590
or something along those lines, right?

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:29.960
But again, this is not
something we maybe we think of

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:30.860
when we think of data viz,

00:06:30.860 --> 00:06:33.480
but it is in a sense

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:36.040
a way to distill down a
lot of complicated data

00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:41.040
into something that can
potentially build policy off of,

00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:43.963
or for COVID response.

00:06:45.040 --> 00:06:47.160
You can have tables with data,

00:06:47.160 --> 00:06:48.960
but you can also distill things down.

00:06:48.960 --> 00:06:49.793
Very commonly,

00:06:49.793 --> 00:06:52.753
we saw a lot of bar charts
this year for COVID,

00:06:54.930 --> 00:06:56.930
kind of comparing numbers across States,

00:06:56.930 --> 00:06:58.963
across countries, across regions.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:03.530
So, for example,

00:07:03.530 --> 00:07:07.460
Trump in an interview for
Axios showed a bar chart

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:10.700
of deaths per cases.

00:07:10.700 --> 00:07:13.723
So this is what was known
as the case fatality rate.

00:07:15.490 --> 00:07:17.830
And, I don't know if you can
see this entirely well here,

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:22.280
but he's showing a bar chart
with four countries on there.

00:07:22.280 --> 00:07:25.230
Obviously, there are more
countries in the world than four,

00:07:26.660 --> 00:07:28.730
but he was showing this to indicate

00:07:28.730 --> 00:07:30.503
that of these four that he showed,

00:07:31.410 --> 00:07:35.680
United States had the
lowest case fatality rate,

00:07:35.680 --> 00:07:36.870
which is fine.

00:07:36.870 --> 00:07:39.650
That is probably true for
the four things he's showing,

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:42.150
but an important thing to note

00:07:42.150 --> 00:07:45.760
is that the case fatality
rate is actually not helpful

00:07:47.197 --> 00:07:48.663
in comparing countries.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:51.730
And again, it's only
showing four countries.

00:07:53.460 --> 00:07:56.040
Governor DeSantis, here from Florida,

00:07:56.040 --> 00:07:57.700
also showed a similar metric.

00:07:57.700 --> 00:08:00.620
In this case, he's actually
comparing different States

00:08:00.620 --> 00:08:03.190
and the case fatality rate again.

00:08:03.190 --> 00:08:06.373
Again, this is deaths by cases,

00:08:07.570 --> 00:08:10.030
but for long-term care
facilities in a given state

00:08:10.030 --> 00:08:13.350
with Florida being this very low one.

00:08:13.350 --> 00:08:14.630
So, right?

00:08:14.630 --> 00:08:18.390
Cases by deaths

00:08:18.390 --> 00:08:22.300
is a metric to measure like many other.

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:25.573
You can kind of create a
fraction of whatever you want,

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:29.590
but a relevant statistic
would be something

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:32.250
that would normalize across
the different states, right?

00:08:32.250 --> 00:08:34.660
The biggest difference
across different states

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:36.240
is the number of people in there.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:38.630
So a simple way to kind of compare

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:41.050
across different places and regions

00:08:41.050 --> 00:08:43.160
is to normalize it by population.

00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:44.530
So a relevant statistic in this case,

00:08:44.530 --> 00:08:46.260
instead of the case fatality rate,

00:08:46.260 --> 00:08:50.900
would be a mortality rate
which is deaths by population.

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:54.773
And so that example,
again another bar chart,

00:08:54.773 --> 00:08:56.853
this time flipped axes,

00:08:58.000 --> 00:09:00.321
Dr. Birx, part of the White House team

00:09:00.321 --> 00:09:02.383
for the coronavirus response.

00:09:03.720 --> 00:09:04.820
Showing here again,

00:09:04.820 --> 00:09:09.820
so the US having deaths
per a hundred thousand

00:09:10.210 --> 00:09:12.560
and the kind of ranking there as well too,

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:15.933
across, again not every
country, select countries,

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.620
but this would be the kind
of more helpful metric

00:09:20.620 --> 00:09:22.270
in this case.

00:09:22.270 --> 00:09:25.580
And so, direct comparisons,

00:09:25.580 --> 00:09:27.350
again population is not the only thing

00:09:27.350 --> 00:09:29.853
that separates different
countries and places,

00:09:31.290 --> 00:09:32.850
but you have to think of other aspects

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:34.690
like differences in public health systems,

00:09:34.690 --> 00:09:36.700
demographic differences,
maybe a population

00:09:36.700 --> 00:09:38.860
is older on average than another,

00:09:38.860 --> 00:09:40.440
and the policy actions taken

00:09:40.440 --> 00:09:42.240
at different points during
the pandemic, right?

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:45.040
So there are so many
potentially confounding factors

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:47.100
when representing data

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:52.100
and talking about kind of
takeaway points to compare

00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:54.180
across different countries.

00:09:54.180 --> 00:09:55.450
But the problem is,

00:09:55.450 --> 00:09:58.640
here when a government official
shows the visualization,

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:01.700
the stakes are immediately
skyrocketing, right?

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:03.750
The audience typically doesn't think

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:07.170
to parse all the things I'm
kind of rattling on about,

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:08.590
and parse all of the different nuances

00:10:08.590 --> 00:10:09.700
of what they're seeing.

00:10:09.700 --> 00:10:12.490
That's just not the typical experience

00:10:12.490 --> 00:10:14.380
of someone watching a press conference,

00:10:14.380 --> 00:10:17.750
or watching a speech
or something like that.

00:10:17.750 --> 00:10:20.300
The onus really should
not be on the audience

00:10:20.300 --> 00:10:21.330
to sort this out.

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:24.031
It's on the creator of
the viz and the presenter

00:10:24.031 --> 00:10:25.000
to provide context.

00:10:25.000 --> 00:10:28.030
So, obviously this is the
most high stakes situation

00:10:28.030 --> 00:10:30.900
of a politician or a government official,

00:10:30.900 --> 00:10:34.860
talking about COVID in this way.

00:10:34.860 --> 00:10:38.440
But unfortunately, putting
viz out into the public space,

00:10:38.440 --> 00:10:42.680
kind of demands this
sort of responsibility

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:43.893
and requires it really.

00:10:45.340 --> 00:10:48.740
And, we need clear data viz design

00:10:48.740 --> 00:10:51.430
that doesn't mislead or misinform,

00:10:51.430 --> 00:10:53.510
and importantly, it
doesn't add to the noise.

00:10:53.510 --> 00:10:56.270
So, if you're thinking about
making public visualization

00:10:56.270 --> 00:10:57.270
with COVID data,

00:10:57.270 --> 00:10:59.700
you have to first ask yourself,

00:10:59.700 --> 00:11:01.700
does this serve a need for the public

00:11:01.700 --> 00:11:04.570
or uncover new information?

00:11:04.570 --> 00:11:05.670
Because if it doesn't,

00:11:06.560 --> 00:11:08.617
you really have to stop and think like,

00:11:08.617 --> 00:11:11.727
"Maybe, I can work on
making a viz for myself,

00:11:11.727 --> 00:11:13.920
"making a viz to like practice,"

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:15.950
which is always awesome thing to do

00:11:15.950 --> 00:11:19.513
'cause a lot of this stuff
takes time to get good at,

00:11:21.380 --> 00:11:24.050
but really think about
where you're sharing

00:11:24.050 --> 00:11:27.170
and how things can be perceived
in a fully public sphere,

00:11:27.170 --> 00:11:29.610
like something like social
media, or social media app,

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:30.760
or something like that,

00:11:31.690 --> 00:11:33.070
because people might not perceive it

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:36.720
the way you might have spent
a lot of time thinking about

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:38.593
and all the nuances surrounding.

00:11:39.540 --> 00:11:42.110
The kind of gist of what
you're probably getting

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:46.110
from me talking here is that
this is a hard thing to do

00:11:46.110 --> 00:11:47.350
and get right.

00:11:47.350 --> 00:11:50.980
And I should not make that
seem any easier than it is

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:52.590
because it's really difficult.

00:11:52.590 --> 00:11:55.570
Visualizing and communicating uncertainty

00:11:55.570 --> 00:11:57.590
which is fundamentally
the struggle here, right?

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:02.040
People are confused about
what's happening with pandemic,

00:12:02.040 --> 00:12:03.350
is extremely difficult,

00:12:03.350 --> 00:12:05.890
especially when the audience is primed

00:12:05.890 --> 00:12:06.820
for straightforward answers.

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:08.117
People are like, "Okay."

00:12:08.117 --> 00:12:09.277
"So where's it bad?"

00:12:09.277 --> 00:12:13.050
"Where is it sort of okay to travel?"

00:12:13.050 --> 00:12:15.720
And, people are trying to
operate in their day-to-day lives

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:17.860
and need to have clear answers

00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:22.860
to guide their experience
on a daily basis.

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:25.454
And so this cartoon kind of
like summarizes that a bit.

00:12:25.454 --> 00:12:29.250
It's actually a really
long fleshed out cartoon

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:32.050
that we have the link for on the site,

00:12:32.050 --> 00:12:35.850
but this is from the viz

00:12:35.850 --> 00:12:37.560
and science team at FiveThirtyEight.

00:12:37.560 --> 00:12:39.750
And so here you can see, right?

00:12:39.750 --> 00:12:41.070
People are stuck inside,

00:12:41.070 --> 00:12:42.027
they wanna know like,

00:12:42.027 --> 00:12:44.187
"How long are we gonna be locked in for?"

00:12:45.610 --> 00:12:46.950
And given the sheer amount of data,

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:49.340
it's hard to come up with clear answers,

00:12:49.340 --> 00:12:53.610
especially when data often
doesn't all kinda point

00:12:53.610 --> 00:12:56.443
to one clear answer because
it almost never does.

